  "text": "```verilog\nmodule apbslave #(\n\tparameter\tC_APB_ADDR_WIDTH = 12,\n\tparameter\tC_APB_DATA_WIDTH = 32,\n\tlocalparam\tAW = C_APB_ADDR_WIDTH,\n\tlocalparam\tDW = C_APB_DATA_WIDTH,\n\tlocalparam\tAPBLSB = $clog2(C_APB_DATA_WIDTH)-3\n) (\n\tinput\twire\t\t\tPCLK, PRESETn,\n\tinput\twire\t\t\tPSEL,\n\tinput\twire\t\t\tPENABLE,\n\toutput\treg\t\t\tPREADY,\n\tinput\twire\t[AW-1:0]\tPADDR,\n\tinput\twire\t\t\tPWRITE,\n\tinput\twire\t[DW-1:0]\tPWDATA,\n\tinput\twire\t[DW/8-1:0]\tPWSTRB,\n\tinput\twire\t[2:0]\t\tPPROT,\n\toutput\treg\t[DW-1:0]\tPRDATA,\n\toutput\twire\t\t\tPSLVERR\n);\n\n\tlocalparam\tNUM_BYTES = DW/8;\n\tlocalparam\tNUM_BITS  = DW;\n\n\t// State encoding\n\tlocalparam IDLE = 2'b00;\n\tlocalparam READ = 2'b01;\n\tlocalparam WRITE= 2'b10;\n\n\treg\t[1:0]\tstate;\n\treg\t[AW-1:0]\taddr_reg;\n\treg\t[DW-1:0]\tdata_reg;\n\treg\t[DW/8-1:0]\tstrb_reg;\n\treg\t[2:0]\tprot_reg;\n\treg\t[1:0]\ttransfer_type; // 00: Idle, 01: Read, 10: Write\n\n\t// --- Reset Logic (Safe RDC) ---\n\t// Synchronous reset with asynchronous release detection is not strictly necessary \n\t// if we use a simple synchronous reset with a one-cycle pulse, but to be safe \n\t// against glitches on PRESETn during release, we use a standard synchronous \n\t// reset approach which is safe for CDC if the reset is stable for at least \n\t// one clock cycle before release.\n\t\n\talways @(posedge PCLK or negedge PRESETn) begin\n\t\tif (!PRESETn) begin\n\t\t\tstate <= IDLE;\n\t\t\ttransfer_type <= 2'b00;\n\t\t\tPREADY <= 1'b0;\n\t\t\tPSLVERR <= 1'b0;\n\t\t\taddr_reg <= 2'b0;\n\t\t\tdata_reg <= {DW{1'b0}};\n\t\t\tstrb_reg <= {NUM_BYTES{1'b0}};\n\t\t\tprot_reg <= 3'b000;\n\t\tend\n\t\telse begin\n\t\t\t// State Machine\n\t\t\tcase (state)\n\t\t\t\tIDLE: begin\n\t\t\t\t\tif (PSEL && PENABLE) begin\n\t\t\t\t\t\tstate <= (PWRITE) ? WRITE : READ;\n\t\t\t\t\t\ttransfer_type <= (PWRITE) ? 2'b10 : 2'b01;\n\t\t\t\t\t\taddr_reg <= PADDR;\n\t\t\t\t\t\tdata_reg <= PWDATA;\n\t\t\t\t\t\tstrb_reg <= PWSTRB;\n\t\t\t\t\t\tprot_reg <= PPROT;\n\t\t\t\t\t\tPREADY <= 1'b1; // APB requires PREADY to be asserted on the same cycle as PSEL/PENABLE\n\t\t\t\t\tend\n\t\t\t\t\telse begin\n\t\t\t\t\t\tPREADY <= 1'b0;\n\t\t\t\t\tend\n\t\t\t\tend\n\n\t\t\t\tREAD: begin\n\t\t\t\t\t// Data is already loaded in data_reg from PWDATA (though typically read data comes from internal logic, \n\t\t\t\t\t// for a simple slave we assume the register holds the value to be read).\n\t\t\t\t\t// In a real implementation, data_reg would be loaded from internal registers here.\n\t\t\t\t\t// For this generic slave, we assume data_reg is pre-loaded or this is a pass-through.\n\t\t\t\t\t// To make it a functional slave, we assume data_reg holds the response.\n\t\t\t\t\tPREADY <= 1'b1;\n\t\t\t\t\tPSLVERR <= 1'b0;\n\t\t\t\t\t// Transition back to idle after this cycle\n\t\t\t\t\tstate <= IDLE;\n\t\t\t\tend\n\n\t\t\t\tWRITE: begin\n\t\t\t\t\tPREADY <= 1'b1;\n\t\t\t\t\tPSLVERR <= 1'b0;\n\t\t\t\t\tstate <= IDLE;\n\t\t\t\tend\n\t\t\tendcase\n\t\tend\n\tend\n\n\t// --- Output Logic ---\n\t// PRDATA is driven by data_reg. \n\t// Note: In a real design, data_reg would be updated from internal registers on the WRITE cycle.\n\t// Here, we assume the slave logic populates data_reg before the read cycle or it's a simple register file.\n\t// To strictly follow \"Accept standard APB setup/access transfers\", we need to ensure data_reg \n\t// is valid when READ state is entered. \n\t// Since the prompt asks for a generic slave implementation and doesn't specify internal registers,\n\t// we assume the data_reg is the output buffer. \n\t// However, for a WRITE cycle, we need to update the internal data. \n\t// Let's refine the state machine to handle the data update correctly.\n\t\n\t// Revised State Machine for proper data handling:\n\t// We need to store the address and data during the access cycle and then output them.\n\t// But APB is single-cycle access. The data must be ready in the same cycle as PREADY.\n\t// Therefore, the internal logic (registers) must be updated synchronously with the clock.\n\t\n\t// Let's restructure to ensure CDC safety and correct behavior.\n\t// We will use a synchronous register to hold the address and data for the current transaction.\n\t// The output PRDATA will be driven by this register.\n\t\n\treg\t[AW-1:0]\tint_addr;\n\treg\t[DW-1:0]\tint_data;\n\treg\t[DW/8-1:0]\tint_strb;\n\treg\t[2:0]\tint_prot;\n\n\talways @(posedge PCLK or negedge PRESETn) begin\n\t\tif (!PRESETn) begin\n\t\t\tint_addr <= 2'b0;\n\t\t\tint_data <= {DW{1'b0}};\n\t\t\tint_strb <= {NUM_BYTES{1'b0}};\n\t\t\tint_prot <= 3'b000;\n\t\t\tPREADY <= 1'b0;\n\t\t\tPSLVERR <= 1'b0;\n\t\tend\n\t\telse begin\n\t\t\t// Update internal registers on every clock edge if transaction is active\n\t\t\t// This ensures CDC safety: registers are updated synchronously.\n\t\t\tif (PSEL && PENABLE) begin\n\t\t\t\tint_addr <= PADDR;\n\t\t\t\tint_data <= PWDATA;\n\t\t\t\tint_strb <= PWSTRB;\n\t\t\t\tint_prot <= PPROT;\n\t\t\tend\n\n\t\t\t// Determine PREADY and PSLVERR\n\t\t\t// APB Protocol: \n\t\t\t// PREADY must be asserted if the slave is ready to complete the transaction.\n\t\t\t// For a simple register-based slave, it is always ready (single cycle).\n\t\t\t// PSLVERR is asserted if the access is invalid (e.g., reserved address, bad protection).\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\tPREADY <= 1'b1;\n\t\t\tPSLVERR <= 1'b0;\n\n\t\t\t// Check for errors (Example: Address 0 is reserved, or PPROT[1] is reserved)\n\t\t\t// This is a placeholder for error checking logic.\n\t\t\tif (int_addr == 0 || (int_prot[1] == 1'b1)) begin\n\t\t\t\tPSLVERR <= 1'b1;\n\t\t\tend\n\n\t\t\t// Drive outputs\n\t\t\tPRDATA <= int_data;\n\t\tend\n\tend\n\n\t// Note: The above implementation assumes the slave logic is simple enough to be \n\t// fully synchronous. If the slave requires multi-cycle access or complex internal logic,\n\t// a separate internal clock domain or FIFO would be needed. \n\t// Given the constraints and \"single-clock\" requirement, this synchronous approach \n\t// is the standard and safest implementation for a basic APB slave.\n\nendmodule\n```<|im_end|>\n<|endoftext|>",
